{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:BringsSurface.SwPeriodResidueSum","@type":"LeanModule","name":"BringsSurface.SwPeriodResidueSum","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Complex.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Fintype.Card"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.total_residue_ne_zero_of_period_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.total_residue_ne_zero_of_period_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/brings-surface/sw-period-residue-sum.lean#L112-L129","doc":"**Contrapositive — what a nonzero period costs.** If the SW period is\nnonzero at some jet level, then the residues appearing in `period_eq` do\n**not** sum to zero. Since the residues of a meromorphic 1-form over *all* its\npoles on a compact surface *do* sum to zero, the sum in `period_eq` cannot\nrange over all of them: it must range over the poles enclosed by a specific\ncontour, a proper subset.\n\nNaming that subset is the missing geometric content of the keystone — and note\nit is not a technicality about contours. The pole locus is the branch locus of\nthe colour cubic, which is **exactly 6 points**, independent of `n`\n(`disc_X = −16z⁶ − 24z⁵ − 35z⁴ − 50z³ − 35z² − 24z − 16`, squarefree; three\nconjugate pairs at `|z| ≈ 0.943, 1.000, 1.061`). Any reading on which the\nresidue family at level `n` is indexed one-to-one by the Wedderburn blocks\n`λ ⊢ n` therefore needs `p(n) ≤ 6`, which fails from `n = 5` (`p(5) = 7`,\n`p(6) = 11`, `p(n) → ∞`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.period_eq_zero_of_total_residue_vanishes","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.period_eq_zero_of_total_residue_vanishes","kind":"theorem","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/brings-surface/sw-period-residue-sum.lean#L89-L110","doc":"**The residue theorem's actual content, and the obstruction it creates.**\n\nOn a compact Riemann surface the residues of a meromorphic 1-form sum to\nzero. Read `period_eq` with its sum ranging over *all* poles — which is how\n`prop:sw-period-residue-sum` states it, `∑_{p ∈ S_c}` — and the period is\ntherefore **zero at every jet level**, whatever the residues individually are.\n\nThat is fatal to the keystone, which needs `a_D^{(n)} = lw(n−1) > 0`\n(`lw(1) = 1`, `lw(2) = (q+1)²/(3q)`, `lw(3) = 5/2 + (3/4)h²`, all positive and\nderived from the q-hook-length formula in `QOU.Mass.QHookDimension`).\n\n**This is a second route to a conclusion the corpus already holds.**\n`prop:sw-classical-jet-exactness` reaches `period = 0` from *exactness*\n(`λ_SW^{(n)} = d(X^{(n−1)})`, so every residue vanishes individually) and\nscopes it as a scope-(b) exclusion of the classical-jet reading. This lemma\nassumes far less — only that the total residue vanishes, not that each one\ndoes — so it survives readings in which individual residues are nonzero, and\nit is driven by the *ambient surface* rather than by the jet rule. The two\nagree, by different mechanisms. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.sw_period_eq_residue_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.sw_period_eq_residue_sum","kind":"theorem","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/brings-surface/sw-period-residue-sum.lean#L73-L87","doc":"**L1.** The Seiberg–Witten period is `2πi` times the sum of its\nresidues — an algebraic residue sum.\n\n**This is a projection, not a derivation**, and is retained only because\ndownstream prose cites `prop:sw-period-residue-sum` by this name: its\nstatement is literally the type of the `period_eq` field, so it assumes\nexactly what it concludes (AGENTS.md §3b-cond item 5). It has been an\nunadjudicated candidate in `docs/audits/lean-triviality.json` since\n2026-08-07. The content of this file is now\n`period_eq_zero_of_total_residue_vanishes` and its contrapositive below,\nwhich *derive* a consequence rather than restating an assumption. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:sw-period-residue-sum","label":"prop:sw-period-residue-sum","md":"quantum-observable-universe/brings-surface/sw-period-residue-sum.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.SWPeriodResidue.period_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.SWPeriodResidue.period_eq","kind":"theorem","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/brings-surface/sw-period-residue-sum.lean#L71-L71","doc":"Residue theorem on the compact surface `S_c ≅ P¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.SWPeriodResidue.twoPiI","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.SWPeriodResidue.twoPiI","kind":"def","line":69,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/brings-surface/sw-period-residue-sum.lean#L69-L69","doc":"The `2πi` normalisation constant of the residue theorem. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.SWPeriodResidue.period","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.SWPeriodResidue.period","kind":"def","line":67,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/brings-surface/sw-period-residue-sum.lean#L67-L67","doc":"The SW period `a_D^{(n)} = ∮ λ_SW^{(n)}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.SWPeriodResidue.residues","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.SWPeriodResidue.residues","kind":"def","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/brings-surface/sw-period-residue-sum.lean#L65-L65","doc":"The residues `Res_p λ_SW^{(n)}` at the `k` branch-point poles. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.SWPeriodResidue.jetLevel","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.SWPeriodResidue.jetLevel","kind":"def","line":63,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/brings-surface/sw-period-residue-sum.lean#L63-L63","doc":"The vertical / jet (body) order `n`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.SWPeriodResidue.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.SWPeriodResidue.mk","kind":"ctor","line":61,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/brings-surface/sw-period-residue-sum.lean#L61-L61","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.SWPeriodResidue","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.SWPeriodResidue","kind":"structure","line":33,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/brings-surface/sw-period-residue-sum.lean#L33-L71","doc":"**SW period / residue data** for the `n`-th prolonged Seiberg–Witten\ndifferential: the finite family of residues at the `k` branch-point\npoles, the period, the `2πi` normalisation, and the identity relating\nthem.\n\nThe geometric *realisation* — that `λ_SW^{(n)}` is meromorphic with\nexactly these residues — is the manuscript content\n(`prop:mi-sw-abelianisation`); here it is packaged as the structure's\nhypothesis so the residue-sum shape is available downstream.\n\n**`period_eq` is a hypothesis, not a consequence of the residue theorem\n(corrected 2026-08-29, bean `qou-6v5l`).** The docstrings and the `.md`\nused to justify it as *\"the residue theorem on the compact Riemann\nsurface `S_c ≅ P¹`\"*. That justification is backwards twice over:\n\n* On a **compact** Riemann surface the residue theorem says the residues\n  of a meromorphic 1-form **sum to zero**. It does not say a period\n  equals the total residue sum — and if the sum in `period_eq` ranges\n  over *all* poles, it forces `period = 0`\n  (`period_eq_zero_of_total_residue_vanishes` below).\n* `P¹(ℂ)` has `H₁ = 0`, so it has **no** generating cycle for a period\n  to be taken over. Only a cycle that *bounds* has a residue-sum value,\n  and then the sum ranges over the **enclosed** poles, a proper subset —\n  which subset is exactly the content the corpus does not yet supply.\n\nSo `period_eq` is a genuine, and genuinely unproved, assumption about\nwhich poles the contour encloses. Keeping it as a structure field is\ncorrect; what was wrong was calling the residue theorem as its warrant. ","signatureChunk":0}]}